::ENG2_U04_L01_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Topic: school gardens. Which research question has the best scope for one assignment?{
=A. How could a school garden support science learning for tenth-grade students during one semester?#Correct. This question is focused by topic, audience, subject area, and time frame.
~B. Are gardens good?#Incorrect. This is too broad and mostly opinion-based.
~C. What is a plant?#Incorrect. This is too narrow and asks for a simple definition.
~D. Why should everyone love gardens forever?#Incorrect. This wording is biased and too broad.
}

::ENG2_U04_L01_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] A student asks, "What about recycling?" What is the main problem with this research question?{
~A. It is too narrow because it can be answered with one fact.#Incorrect. The problem is not that it is too narrow.
=B. It is only a vague topic, not a focused research question.#Correct. It does not ask what the student wants to learn about recycling.
~C. It is already fully refined.#Incorrect. It needs a focus, audience, place, or effect.
~D. It includes too much evidence.#Incorrect. It includes no evidence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L01_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Broad topic: cafeteria food waste. Which revision narrows the topic by solution and time frame?{
~A. Is food waste bad?#Incorrect. This is broad and opinion-based.
~B. What is food?#Incorrect. This is too narrow and asks for a definition.
=C. How could a pre-order lunch form reduce cafeteria food waste during one semester?#Correct. This question names a solution and time frame.
~D. Why is lunch the best class?#Incorrect. This is not a researchable academic question.
}

::ENG2_U04_L01_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] In research, what does "scope" mean?{
~A. The color of a notebook.#Incorrect. Scope is not a physical object.
~B. A source that cannot be trusted.#Incorrect. That relates to reliability, not scope.
~C. A personal opinion about the topic.#Incorrect. Scope is about the limits of inquiry.
=D. The size and limits of a research question.#Correct. Scope tells what the research includes and leaves out.
}

::ENG2_U04_L01_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Question: "How can schools solve every environmental problem?" What is the best refinement?{
~A. Why is the environment important?#Incorrect. This is still too broad.
=B. How could compost bins reduce cafeteria trash at one high school during a semester?#Correct. This narrows the topic by solution, place, and time frame.
~C. Do schools exist?#Incorrect. This is too narrow and not useful for research.
~D. Why is composting obviously perfect?#Incorrect. This is biased and not objective.
}
